ng that failed stays failed in this subtree\" and \"a state's solvability does not depend on how it was reached\").\n200-- **4.4 The repaired search is sound (`s4:285–333`).**\n201-  - Proposition 4.8 and Theorem 4.9 are machinery for Theorem 4.10. Keep Theorem 4.10 in the main text, stated informally first, and move 4.8 and 4.9 with their proofs to the appendix.\n202-  - `s4:319–333`, the validation paragraph, is important: it says what is proved (the model) and what is only tested (the code). Present it as a short list: \"the Lean model; agreement of the code with the model on N checks; a certificate checker\". Move the eight-digit counts to the supplement.\n203-  - The last two sentences (58 lost nodes, \"No whole instance ... is known\") are the stakes. Repeat them at the start of Section 4.\n204-- **4.5 The search in pathwidth language (`s4:335–446`).** Strong content, and the intellectual payoff of the paper.\n205-  - `s4:369–372`: \"Neither line cites the other ...\" is the concrete instance of Section 2.4's finding. Say so explicitly: \"This is the isolation measured in Section 2.4, at the level of one algorithm.\"\n206-  - **\"Prior reports\" (`s4:434–446`)** is a due-diligence paragraph. Shorten it to two sentences and move the reading percentages to the supplement.\n207-- **4.6 The revised algorithm in practice (`s4:448–526`).**\n208-  - **There is no experimental setup.** IJOC requires hardware, implementation language, time limits, instance sources and the meaning of \"one worker\", \"pairs\" and \"descents\".\n209-  - Jargon to remove: `s4:456–458`, \"Since 1 October 2026 these are the default in both of our solvers\"; `s4:473`, \"the MOSP C engine\"; `s4:477`, \"our MOSP corpus\" (never introduced, and its relation to Table 4.6 is unexplained); `s4:479`, \"exact subset-lattice computation, a checked DRAT refutation\" (both undefined); `s4:512`, `csearch` (undefined; it is a configuration name).\n210-  - Certificates (`s4:499–516`): the 55-word sentence at `s4:503–508` should be a bulleted list of what the checker verifies.\n211-- **4.7 Dataset (`s4:528–584`).** Make this its own section.\n212-  - Define provenance in plain words. *Refutation* means proved optimal by exhaustive search. *Bound* means proved optimal because a lower bound meets the witness. *Upper bound* means witnessed but not proved.\n213-  - \"minor certificate that replays\" (`s4:542`) and \"nauty certificates\" (Table 4.6 caption) need a gloss.\n214-  - The \"caution about MOSP benchmark files\" (`s4:572–576`) is useful to practitioners. Keep it.\n215:  - The pricing paragraph (`s4:578–584`) belongs in the closing section's open problems.\n216-\n217:### Section 5, Closing (`s5`, placeholder)\n218-\n219-What it must do, in this order, and in at most a page and a half:\n220-\n221-1. **Answer the central question** in one paragraph:\n222-   - which of the twelve are pathwidth (exact, band, false);\n223-   - what moving between them exposed (a false theorem, already corrected in another literature);\n224-   - what it made possible (one dataset for nine problems).\n225-2. **Say what an OR reader should do differently:**\n226-   - cite the corrected table (Table 3.1), not the 2002 one;\n227-   - look for MOSP and gate matrix results under \"pathwidth\";\n228-   - implementations of Chu and Stuckey's rules (including the code used by Frinhani et al. 2018 and reused by Martin, Yanasse and Pinto 2022) should adopt the matching test. Their values are not known to be wrong, but their refutations are not proved.\n229-3. **State the limits honestly, in one list:**\n230-   - the Lean model and the code are linked by testing and certificates, not by proof;\n231-   - the certificate's acceptance condition is argued on paper;\n232-   - LaPaugh's es = pes is stated but not formalised;\n233-   - one reference of the table is not held;\n234-   - three re-certifications are still running (resolve these before submission if at all possible: \"in flight\" will not survive refereeing).\n235:4. **Open problems.** The placeholder (`s5:9–10`) lists \"the gap between pathwidth and treewidth on trees of cliques, where no degree or clique bound can close it\". **This topic appears nowhere in the body.** Either introduce it in the body (a paragraph in the dataset section on why some classes stay open would do) or leave it out. A closing section must not introduce new material.\n236-\n237----\n238-\n239-## 4. Figures and tables, one by one\n240-\n241-### Table 1.1, the reproduced 2002 table (p. 2, `s1:59–85`)\n242-\n243-- **What it shows:** the twelve problems, their disciplines and references, as printed in 2002.\n244-- **What to conclude:** twelve problems from three fields were claimed equivalent, with references but no stated relations.\n245:- **Does the text or caption say it?** The caption reproduces the original's wording (\"studied independently on the literature\"). The \"on\" is the original's error and reads as the authors' own; mark it as a quotation. The conclusion is stated in prose (`s1:50–51`), not in the caption.\n246-- **Necessary?** Yes; it is the object of the paper.\n247-- **Recommendation:** add a fifth column, visually separated (shaded or after a double rule), headed \"This paper\", with entries such as *exact, = pw+1*, *band*, *false*. This puts the punchline on p. 2 and lets Table 3.1 be the detailed version. Use the same row order in both.\n248-\n249-### Table 2.1, works and citations per name (p. 4, `s2:72–81`)\n250-\n251-- **What it shows:** relevant works, raw hits and citations per name.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
