{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfmXNvKNQpS8dwPabMVnj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014hTxvWhpD5oHfQ7d9zwLN3","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/al/dev/MOSP/paper2/latex && wc -l sec*.tex main.tex && cat sec1_intro.tex && sed -n 1,140p main.tex | grep -n -i -A25 \"begin{abstract}\""},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8202,"cache_read_input_tokens":77811,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8202,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"88e1805d-869e-452b-972d-e62a062bdadf","uuid":"b71a6a1d-1237-44aa-a0ec-70e0ba3fcf13","timestamp":"2026-10-06T19:19:59.143Z","request_id":"req_011CfmXNuxHbkewRDzgbuuvT","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_014hTxvWhpD5oHfQ7d9zwLN3":{"command":"cd /home/al/dev/MOSP/paper2/latex && wc -l sec*.tex main.tex && cat sec1_intro.tex && sed -n 1,140p main.tex | grep -n -i -A25 \"begin{abstract}\""}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014hTxvWhpD5oHfQ7d9zwLN3","type":"tool_result","content":"   182 sec1_intro.tex\n   363 sec2_equivalences.tex\n   132 sec3_names.tex\n   564 sec4_search.tex\n   252 sec5_results.tex\n    22 sec6_conclusion.tex\n    38 sec7_data.tex\n   117 sec9a_appendix_lean.tex\n   177 sec9b_appendix_machinery.tex\n    95 sec9c_appendix_names.tex\n   139 main.tex\n  2081 total\n% Section 1. Rewritten 2026-10-06 (loop0009 item 02) after\n% paper2/review_readability.md section 7 and the owner's notes in\n% Ralph_Loops/loop0009/TASK.md. The history paragraph keeps the owner's account\n% of how the thesis's Table 2.1 was assembled (first version written the same\n% day from literature/linhares_2001_phd_thesis_industrial_pattern_sequencing.pdf).\n% Figure 1.1: python -m paper2.intro_figure (numbers checked by\n% tests/test_intro_figure.py). Counts of rows: Table 2.1 (tab:master).\n% Headline numbers: Sections 3-5 and their sources there.\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n\nA factory makes a set of patterns, one at a time, and each customer's order\nneeds some of them. A customer's stack opens when the first pattern the\ncustomer needs is made, and it closes when the last one is made. The\n\\emph{minimization of open stacks problem} (MOSP) asks for the order of the\npatterns that keeps the fewest stacks open at any one time. In the cutting\nindustry, where the problem arose, the patterns are cutting patterns and the\ncustomers are piece types \\citep{Yanasse1997b}. Figure~\\ref{fig:example}(a)\ngives a small instance. Six customers, $a$ to $f$, need four patterns:\n$P_1=\\{a,b\\}$, $P_2=\\{b,c,d\\}$, $P_3=\\{c,d,e\\}$ and $P_4=\\{e,f\\}$. Made in\norder A, $P_1P_2P_3P_4$, the patterns keep at most 3 stacks open. Made in order\nB, $P_1P_3P_4P_2$, they keep 5 open, because customer $b$ is served first but\nwaits for $P_2$, which comes last. Of the 24 orders, 2 reach 3 (order A and its\nreverse), 16 give 4 and 6 give 5. MOSP is NP-hard\n\\citep{Linhares2001,LY2002}.\n\n\\begin{figure}[tbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{sec1_fig1_example.pdf}\n\\caption{One instance, three readings, one number. (a) Six customers and four\npatterns, with each customer's open stack shaded in order A; order A keeps at\nmost 3 stacks open and order B at most 5, and only 2 of the 24 orders reach 3.\n(b, c) The same matrix as a gate matrix layout: the same two orders need 3 and\n5 tracks (the shaded gate is where the density peaks). (d) The same matrix as a\ngraph, one clique per pattern: the open sets of order A are the bags of a path\ndecomposition of width 2, so the optimum is the pathwidth plus one\n(Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp}).}\n\\label{fig:example}\n\\end{figure}\n\nThe same matrix is a problem in VLSI design. In a \\emph{gate matrix layout}\nthe patterns are gates, drawn as vertical lines in some order, and the\ncustomers are nets. Each net is a horizontal wire from the first gate it\nconnects to the last, and wires that overlap must lie on different horizontal\ntracks. The task is to order the gates so that the fewest tracks suffice.\nFigure~\\ref{fig:example}(b) and (c) draw the two orders. The wires are the same;\nonly the gate order changes which wires overlap. Order A packs into 3 tracks\nand order B needs 5. The number of tracks a gate order needs is its\n\\emph{density}, the most wires crossing any one gate, and that is exactly the\nnumber of open stacks there. So the two problems compute the same number from\nthe same matrix \\citep[Proposition~2]{LY2002}.\n\nThe same matrix is also a graph. Make each customer a vertex, and join two\ncustomers when some pattern needs both, so that each pattern becomes a clique\n(Figure~\\ref{fig:example}(d)). Write down, for order A, the set of open stacks\nat each position: $\\{a,b\\}$, $\\{b,c,d\\}$, $\\{c,d,e\\}$, $\\{e,f\\}$. These sets\ncover every vertex and every edge, and each customer appears in a run of\nconsecutive sets. In graph theory such a sequence is a \\emph{path\ndecomposition}, its sets are \\emph{bags}, and its width is the size of its\nlargest bag minus one. The least width over all path decompositions is the\n\\emph{pathwidth} of the graph, here 2. The construction works in both\ndirections, so the fewest open stacks is always the pathwidth plus one\n(Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp}).\n\n\\begin{table}[tbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Twelve problems from three fields, published as one family: eight\nrows are exact, counting the pathwidth row itself; two are bands; two are false\nas the table states them. The Linhares--Yanasse table, reproduced from\n\\citet[Table~1, p.~1764]{LY2002} with its row order, wording and references as\nprinted, and its caption: ``Display of a family of equivalent problems studied\nindependently on the literature''. The shaded column is ours. It gives the\nrelation to pathwidth ($\\pw$) that is true, proved in Section~\\ref{sec:equiv}\n(Table~\\ref{tab:master}).}\n\\label{tab:ly2002}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll>{\\columncolor{black!7}}l@{}}\n\\toprule\nProblem & Discipline & References & This paper \\\\\n\\midrule\nMOSP & Operations research & \\citet{Yanasse1997b}; \\citet{LY2002ref4} & exact: $\\pw+1$ \\\\\nGate matrix layout & VLSI design & \\citet{LY2002ref6}; \\citet{LY2002ref8} & exact: $\\pw+1$ \\\\\nOne-dimensional logic & VLSI design & \\citet{LY2002ref7} & exact: $\\pw+1$ \\\\\nPLA folding & VLSI design & \\citet{LY2002ref6} & false$^{a}$ \\\\\nInterval thickness & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref5} & exact: $\\pw+1$ \\\\\nNode search game & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref9} & exact: $\\pw+1$ \\\\\nEdge search game & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref10} & band: $\\pw$ to $\\pw+2$ \\\\\nNarrowness & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref11} & exact: $\\pw+1$ \\\\\nSplit bandwidth & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref12} & band: $\\pw$ to $\\pw+1$ \\\\\nGraph path-width & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref13} & exact: $\\pw$ (reference) \\\\\nEdge separation & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref14} & false$^{b}$ \\\\\nVertex separation & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref13} & exact: $\\pw$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\multicolumn{4}{@{}p{\\linewidth}@{}}{\\footnotesize $^{a}$Exact ($\\pw+1$) for\nmultiple folding, with any number of nets per track.\n$^{b}$As cutwidth; the vertex separator game of the same reference is exact\n($\\pw$, for graphs with an edge).}\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\citet{LY2002} collected twelve such problems, from operations research, VLSI\ndesign and graph theory, in the table that Table~\\ref{tab:ly2002} reproduces.\nEach computes, on corresponding inputs, ``a function $f(\\Pi)$ that is either\nequal to the number of open stacks or closely related to it (plus or minus\none)'' \\citep[p.~1764]{LY2002}. The table gives a reference for each problem,\nbut no proofs and no graph of which problem reduces to which. ``Plus or minus\none'' has two readings. Under the \\emph{literal} reading, $f$ is within one of\nthe number of open stacks on every input. Under the \\emph{fixed-offset}\nreading, $f$ equals the number of open stacks plus one constant, the same on\nevery input. Only the second makes solving one problem the same as solving\nanother. We call the twelve \\emph{the pathwidth complex}, because pathwidth is\nboth the name the literature uses most (Section~\\ref{sec:names}) and the\nquantity through which every true relation in the table is proved\n(Section~\\ref{sec:equiv}). This paper asks: \\emph{which of the equalities in\nthe Linhares--Yanasse table are true, and what follows when results,\nalgorithms and benchmarks are moved between the twelve problems through\npathwidth?}\n\nThe table has a history that explains why the question is still open. It was\nfirst printed in the first author's doctoral thesis\n\\citep[Table~2.1, p.~13]{Linhares2001}, supervised by the second author, which proved MOSP\nNP-hard and equal to gate matrix layout. Its rows were collected over the years\nof the doctorate, while reading the literature of each field. Each came with a\nreference and nothing else. No row's relation to open stacks was checked, no\npublished proof was restated, and no graph said which problem reduces to which.\nThe table appeared again, row for row, as Table~1 of \\citet{LY2002}, and it has\nbeen read since as a statement of equivalence.\n\\citet{GarciaDeLaBandaStuckey2007}, for instance, credit the speed of their\nsearch in this journal to ``the fact that the problem is fixed parameter\ntractable (Linhares and Yanasse 2002)''. So the table is already used to\njustify algorithms, and whether it is true matters. If it is true, every result\nabout any one of the twelve problems transfers to all the others. Looking back,\nthe table was the thesis's most important finding, and this article does what\nthe thesis did not: it states each problem precisely, proves what is true and\nrefutes what is not. The communities that could have checked the table\nrarely read each other: of 844 works that cite the table's papers,\nonly six cite both an open-stacks paper and a graph-theory paper\n(Section~\\ref{sec:names}).\n\n\\paragraph{What we find.}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\emph{The table, settled.} Eight of the twelve rows are exact, the\n  pathwidth row itself counted as one of them: the other seven equal pathwidth\n  or pathwidth plus one on every input, outside degenerate cases such as an\n  empty graph. Two rows, split bandwidth and edge\n  search, lie in bands one and two wide. Two rows, PLA folding and edge\n  separation, are false as stated, with counterexamples. A variant of each\n  false row is exact (Section~\\ref{sec:equiv}).\n\\item \\emph{One name.} The literature has settled on ``pathwidth'', which has\n  more than three times as many relevant works as the other eleven names\n  together. Besides pathwidth, the most active community over 2005--2024 is the\n  open-stacks one, and the exact search of Section~\\ref{sec:search} came\n  from it\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:names}).\n\\item \\emph{A repaired search.} The best exact algorithm for MOSP, the search of\n  \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, is also a pathwidth algorithm. Two of its published\n  dominance theorems are false, as are their later restatements\n  \\citep{Chu2011,Fink2012}. The correct rule was already known in the exact\n  pathwidth literature, and we show that it can be tested by one bipartite\n  matching per candidate. The repaired search is proved sound. Over each\n  benchmark set it changes the total number of search nodes by at most 2\\%, and no wrong answer of the\n  published rules on a whole instance is known (Section~\\ref{sec:search}).\n\\item \\emph{Benchmarks re-certified, and a dataset.} The repaired search\n  re-proved all 115 benchmark optima that rested on the published rules, and\n  none changed (as of 6 October 2026). We release 17,714 graphs, distinct up to\n  isomorphism, from twenty benchmark collections of the twelve problems; 16,087\n  have a certified pathwidth with a layout that attains it, and so a certified\n  value for every exact problem in the table (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\paragraph{How to read the paper.} Section~\\ref{sec:equiv} states the twelve\nproblems and proves what is true. Section~\\ref{sec:names} measures which names\nand communities the literature uses. Section~\\ref{sec:search} reads the exact\nopen-stacks search as a pathwidth algorithm, finds the two false rules and\nproves the repaired search sound. Section~\\ref{sec:results} reports its cost\nand the dataset, and Section~\\ref{sec:closing} answers the question above.\nEvery theorem we state is machine-checked in the Lean~4 proof assistant. The\nreader need not read Lean: the proofs are given in the text, and\nAppendix~\\ref{app:lean} maps each result to its formal statement.\nAppendix~\\ref{app:machinery-all} holds the supporting material,\nAppendix~\\ref{app:names} the bibliometric method, and the data,\ncode and proofs are described at the end of the paper (page~\\pageref{sec:data}).\n99:\\begin{abstract}\n100-\\noindent\n101-The minimization of open stacks problem (MOSP) asks for the order in which to\n102-make a set of patterns so that the fewest customer orders are open at once.\n103-\\citet{LY2002} listed twelve problems from operations research, VLSI design and\n104-graph theory, MOSP among them, as equal to it ``plus or minus one'', and gave\n105-no proofs. We ask which of these equalities are true, and what follows when\n106-results, algorithms and benchmarks are moved between the twelve problems\n107-through pathwidth, the quantity that connects them. We find four things. First,\n108-eight of the twelve rows are exact, counting pathwidth itself: each equals\n109-pathwidth or pathwidth plus one. Two lie in bands one and two wide, and two are\n110-false as stated; every relation is proved, and machine-checked in Lean.\n111-Second, the literature has settled on the name pathwidth, used more than three\n112-times as often as the other eleven names together, and the three communities\n113-rarely cite one another. Third, the best exact algorithm for MOSP, the search of\n114-\\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, is a pathwidth algorithm, and two of its published\n115-dominance theorems are false. The correct rule was already known for\n116-pathwidth; we show that it is tested by one bipartite matching per candidate,\n117-and prove the repaired search sound. In our implementation it costs at most\n118-2\\% more search nodes on each benchmark set, and it re-proved all 115 benchmark\n119-optima that rested on the published rules, changing none. Fourth, we release\n120-17,714 benchmark graphs, distinct up to isomorphism, from twenty collections,\n121-16,087 of them with a certified pathwidth and a layout that attains it.\n122-\\end{abstract}\n123-\n124-\\input{sec1_intro}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"88e1805d-869e-452b-972d-e62a062bdadf","uuid":"9b5da6cb-c7b6-4218-b33b-87b47e9cbd86","timestamp":"2026-10-06T19:19:59.174Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"   182 sec1_intro.tex\n   363 sec2_equivalences.tex\n   132 sec3_names.tex\n   564 sec4_search.tex\n   252 sec5_results.tex\n    22 sec6_conclusion.tex\n    38 sec7_data.tex\n   117 sec9a_appendix_lean.tex\n   177 sec9b_appendix_machinery.tex\n    95 sec9c_appendix_names.tex\n   139 main.tex\n  2081 total\n% Section 1. Rewritten 2026-10-06 (loop0009 item 02) after\n% paper2/review_readability.md section 7 and the owner's notes in\n% Ralph_Loops/loop0009/TASK.md. The history paragraph keeps the owner's account\n% of how the thesis's Table 2.1 was assembled (first version written the same\n% day from literature/linhares_2001_phd_thesis_industrial_pattern_sequencing.pdf).\n% Figure 1.1: python -m paper2.intro_figure (numbers checked by\n% tests/test_intro_figure.py). Counts of rows: Table 2.1 (tab:master).\n% Headline numbers: Sections 3-5 and their sources there.\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n\nA factory makes a set of patterns, one at a time, and each customer's order\nneeds some of them. A customer's stack opens when the first pattern the\ncustomer needs is made, and it closes when the last one is made. The\n\\emph{minimization of open stacks problem} (MOSP) asks for the order of the\npatterns that keeps the fewest stacks open at any one time. In the cutting\nindustry, where the problem arose, the patterns are cutting patterns and the\ncustomers are piece types \\citep{Yanasse1997b}. Figure~\\ref{fig:example}(a)\ngives a small instance. Six customers, $a$ to $f$, need four patterns:\n$P_1=\\{a,b\\}$, $P_2=\\{b,c,d\\}$, $P_3=\\{c,d,e\\}$ and $P_4=\\{e,f\\}$. Made in\norder A, $P_1P_2P_3P_4$, the patterns keep at most 3 stacks open. Made in order\nB, $P_1P_3P_4P_2$, they keep 5 open, because customer $b$ is served first but\nwaits for $P_2$, which comes last. Of the 24 orders, 2 reach 3 (order A and its\nreverse), 16 give 4 and 6 give 5. MOSP is NP-hard\n\\citep{Linhares2001,LY2002}.\n\n\\begin{figure}[tbp]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{sec1_fig1_example.pdf}\n\\caption{One instance, three readings, one number. (a) Six customers and four\npatterns, with each customer's open stack shaded in order A; order A keeps at\nmost 3 stacks open and order B at most 5, and only 2 of the 24 orders reach 3.\n(b, c) The same matrix as a gate matrix layout: the same two orders need 3 and\n5 tracks (the shaded gate is where the density peaks). (d) The same matrix as a\ngraph, one clique per pattern: the open sets of order A are the bags of a path\ndecomposition of width 2, so the optimum is the pathwidth plus one\n(Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp}).}\n\\label{fig:example}\n\\end{figure}\n\nThe same matrix is a problem in VLSI design. In a \\emph{gate matrix layout}\nthe patterns are gates, drawn as vertical lines in some order, and the\ncustomers are nets. Each net is a horizontal wire from the first gate it\nconnects to the last, and wires that overlap must lie on different horizontal\ntracks. The task is to order the gates so that the fewest tracks suffice.\nFigure~\\ref{fig:example}(b) and (c) draw the two orders. The wires are the same;\nonly the gate order changes which wires overlap. Order A packs into 3 tracks\nand order B needs 5. The number of tracks a gate order needs is its\n\\emph{density}, the most wires crossing any one gate, and that is exactly the\nnumber of open stacks there. So the two problems compute the same number from\nthe same matrix \\citep[Proposition~2]{LY2002}.\n\nThe same matrix is also a graph. Make each customer a vertex, and join two\ncustomers when some pattern needs both, so that each pattern becomes a clique\n(Figure~\\ref{fig:example}(d)). Write down, for order A, the set of open stacks\nat each position: $\\{a,b\\}$, $\\{b,c,d\\}$, $\\{c,d,e\\}$, $\\{e,f\\}$. These sets\ncover every vertex and every edge, and each customer appears in a run of\nconsecutive sets. In graph theory such a sequence is a \\emph{path\ndecomposition}, its sets are \\emph{bags}, and its width is the size of its\nlargest bag minus one. The least width over all path decompositions is the\n\\emph{pathwidth} of the graph, here 2. The construction works in both\ndirections, so the fewest open stacks is always the pathwidth plus one\n(Theorem~\\ref{thm:mosp}).\n\n\\begin{table}[tbp]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Twelve problems from three fields, published as one family: eight\nrows are exact, counting the pathwidth row itself; two are bands; two are false\nas the table states them. The Linhares--Yanasse table, reproduced from\n\\citet[Table~1, p.~1764]{LY2002} with its row order, wording and references as\nprinted, and its caption: ``Display of a family of equivalent problems studied\nindependently on the literature''. The shaded column is ours. It gives the\nrelation to pathwidth ($\\pw$) that is true, proved in Section~\\ref{sec:equiv}\n(Table~\\ref{tab:master}).}\n\\label{tab:ly2002}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lll>{\\columncolor{black!7}}l@{}}\n\\toprule\nProblem & Discipline & References & This paper \\\\\n\\midrule\nMOSP & Operations research & \\citet{Yanasse1997b}; \\citet{LY2002ref4} & exact: $\\pw+1$ \\\\\nGate matrix layout & VLSI design & \\citet{LY2002ref6}; \\citet{LY2002ref8} & exact: $\\pw+1$ \\\\\nOne-dimensional logic & VLSI design & \\citet{LY2002ref7} & exact: $\\pw+1$ \\\\\nPLA folding & VLSI design & \\citet{LY2002ref6} & false$^{a}$ \\\\\nInterval thickness & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref5} & exact: $\\pw+1$ \\\\\nNode search game & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref9} & exact: $\\pw+1$ \\\\\nEdge search game & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref10} & band: $\\pw$ to $\\pw+2$ \\\\\nNarrowness & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref11} & exact: $\\pw+1$ \\\\\nSplit bandwidth & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref12} & band: $\\pw$ to $\\pw+1$ \\\\\nGraph path-width & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref13} & exact: $\\pw$ (reference) \\\\\nEdge separation & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref14} & false$^{b}$ \\\\\nVertex separation & Graph theory & \\citet{LY2002ref13} & exact: $\\pw$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\multicolumn{4}{@{}p{\\linewidth}@{}}{\\footnotesize $^{a}$Exact ($\\pw+1$) for\nmultiple folding, with any number of nets per track.\n$^{b}$As cutwidth; the vertex separator game of the same reference is exact\n($\\pw$, for graphs with an edge).}\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\citet{LY2002} collected twelve such problems, from operations research, VLSI\ndesign and graph theory, in the table that Table~\\ref{tab:ly2002} reproduces.\nEach computes, on corresponding inputs, ``a function $f(\\Pi)$ that is either\nequal to the number of open stacks or closely related to it (plus or minus\none)'' \\citep[p.~1764]{LY2002}. The table gives a reference for each problem,\nbut no proofs and no graph of which problem reduces to which. ``Plus or minus\none'' has two readings. Under the \\emph{literal} reading, $f$ is within one of\nthe number of open stacks on every input. Under the \\emph{fixed-offset}\nreading, $f$ equals the number of open stacks plus one constant, the same on\nevery input. Only the second makes solving one problem the same as solving\nanother. We call the twelve \\emph{the pathwidth complex}, because pathwidth is\nboth the name the literature uses most (Section~\\ref{sec:names}) and the\nquantity through which every true relation in the table is proved\n(Section~\\ref{sec:equiv}). This paper asks: \\emph{which of the equalities in\nthe Linhares--Yanasse table are true, and what follows when results,\nalgorithms and benchmarks are moved between the twelve problems through\npathwidth?}\n\nThe table has a history that explains why the question is still open. It was\nfirst printed in the first author's doctoral thesis\n\\citep[Table~2.1, p.~13]{Linhares2001}, supervised by the second author, which proved MOSP\nNP-hard and equal to gate matrix layout. Its rows were collected over the years\nof the doctorate, while reading the literature of each field. Each came with a\nreference and nothing else. No row's relation to open stacks was checked, no\npublished proof was restated, and no graph said which problem reduces to which.\nThe table appeared again, row for row, as Table~1 of \\citet{LY2002}, and it has\nbeen read since as a statement of equivalence.\n\\citet{GarciaDeLaBandaStuckey2007}, for instance, credit the speed of their\nsearch in this journal to ``the fact that the problem is fixed parameter\ntractable (Linhares and Yanasse 2002)''. So the table is already used to\njustify algorithms, and whether it is true matters. If it is true, every result\nabout any one of the twelve problems transfers to all the others. Looking back,\nthe table was the thesis's most important finding, and this article does what\nthe thesis did not: it states each problem precisely, proves what is true and\nrefutes what is not. The communities that could have checked the table\nrarely read each other: of 844 works that cite the table's papers,\nonly six cite both an open-stacks paper and a graph-theory paper\n(Section~\\ref{sec:names}).\n\n\\paragraph{What we find.}\n\\begin{itemize}\n\\item \\emph{The table, settled.} Eight of the twelve rows are exact, the\n  pathwidth row itself counted as one of them: the other seven equal pathwidth\n  or pathwidth plus one on every input, outside degenerate cases such as an\n  empty graph. Two rows, split bandwidth and edge\n  search, lie in bands one and two wide. Two rows, PLA folding and edge\n  separation, are false as stated, with counterexamples. A variant of each\n  false row is exact (Section~\\ref{sec:equiv}).\n\\item \\emph{One name.} The literature has settled on ``pathwidth'', which has\n  more than three times as many relevant works as the other eleven names\n  together. Besides pathwidth, the most active community over 2005--2024 is the\n  open-stacks one, and the exact search of Section~\\ref{sec:search} came\n  from it\n  (Section~\\ref{sec:names}).\n\\item \\emph{A repaired search.} The best exact algorithm for MOSP, the search of\n  \\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, is also a pathwidth algorithm. Two of its published\n  dominance theorems are false, as are their later restatements\n  \\citep{Chu2011,Fink2012}. The correct rule was already known in the exact\n  pathwidth literature, and we show that it can be tested by one bipartite\n  matching per candidate. The repaired search is proved sound. Over each\n  benchmark set it changes the total number of search nodes by at most 2\\%, and no wrong answer of the\n  published rules on a whole instance is known (Section~\\ref{sec:search}).\n\\item \\emph{Benchmarks re-certified, and a dataset.} The repaired search\n  re-proved all 115 benchmark optima that rested on the published rules, and\n  none changed (as of 6 October 2026). We release 17,714 graphs, distinct up to\n  isomorphism, from twenty benchmark collections of the twelve problems; 16,087\n  have a certified pathwidth with a layout that attains it, and so a certified\n  value for every exact problem in the table (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n\\end{itemize}\n\n\\paragraph{How to read the paper.} Section~\\ref{sec:equiv} states the twelve\nproblems and proves what is true. Section~\\ref{sec:names} measures which names\nand communities the literature uses. Section~\\ref{sec:search} reads the exact\nopen-stacks search as a pathwidth algorithm, finds the two false rules and\nproves the repaired search sound. Section~\\ref{sec:results} reports its cost\nand the dataset, and Section~\\ref{sec:closing} answers the question above.\nEvery theorem we state is machine-checked in the Lean~4 proof assistant. The\nreader need not read Lean: the proofs are given in the text, and\nAppendix~\\ref{app:lean} maps each result to its formal statement.\nAppendix~\\ref{app:machinery-all} holds the supporting material,\nAppendix~\\ref{app:names} the bibliometric method, and the data,\ncode and proofs are described at the end of the paper (page~\\pageref{sec:data}).\n99:\\begin{abstract}\n100-\\noindent\n101-The minimization of open stacks problem (MOSP) asks for the order in which to\n102-make a set of patterns so that the fewest customer orders are open at once.\n103-\\citet{LY2002} listed twelve problems from operations research, VLSI design and\n104-graph theory, MOSP among them, as equal to it ``plus or minus one'', and gave\n105-no proofs. We ask which of these equalities are true, and what follows when\n106-results, algorithms and benchmarks are moved between the twelve problems\n107-through pathwidth, the quantity that connects them. We find four things. First,\n108-eight of the twelve rows are exact, counting pathwidth itself: each equals\n109-pathwidth or pathwidth plus one. Two lie in bands one and two wide, and two are\n110-false as stated; every relation is proved, and machine-checked in Lean.\n111-Second, the literature has settled on the name pathwidth, used more than three\n112-times as often as the other eleven names together, and the three communities\n113-rarely cite one another. Third, the best exact algorithm for MOSP, the search of\n114-\\citet{ChuStuckey2009}, is a pathwidth algorithm, and two of its published\n115-dominance theorems are false. The correct rule was already known for\n116-pathwidth; we show that it is tested by one bipartite matching per candidate,\n117-and prove the repaired search sound. In our implementation it costs at most\n118-2\\% more search nodes on each benchmark set, and it re-proved all 115 benchmark\n119-optima that rested on the published rules, changing none. Fourth, we release\n120-17,714 benchmark graphs, distinct up to isomorphism, from twenty collections,\n121-16,087 of them with a certified pathwidth and a layout that attains it.\n122-\\end{abstract}\n123-\n124-\\input{sec1_intro}","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
