{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfmaTXRCN9WRC2vjTMyHB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item 09 is finished: the conclusion is written, the gate passed, and the item is marked `[x]`. That makes 9 of 11 items done, so LOOP_COMPLETE was not added.\n\n**The conclusion (`paper2/latex/sec6_conclusion.tex`, about 1.3 pages)** replaces the placeholder and follows the order the review asks for:\n- **The answer to the central question comes first:** eight rows are exact (counting the pathwidth row itself), two are bands, and two are false, though each false row has an exact variant. Moving between the problems paid off twice: it exposed the false search theorems, and it made one dataset serve nine problems.\n- **What transfers:** within the exact core, everything carries over with its offset. Across a band, only an approximation carries over. Across a false row, nothing does.\n- **What the search teaches about dominance rules:** not seeing wrong answers was never evidence that the rules were sound. The paragraph then covers what bears on that: reading the rules as pathwidth rules, the order the rules run in, and the certificate checker.\n- **What a reader should do differently:** cite the corrected table, look for MOSP results under \"pathwidth\", and adopt the matching test in implementations of Chu and Stuckey's rules.\n- **Limits, as one list:**\n  - the Lean model and the code are linked only by testing and certificates;\n  - the certificate's acceptance condition is argued on paper, not in Lean;\n  - LaPaugh's es = pes is stated but not formalised;\n  - the Kashiwabara & Fujisawa (1979) reference is not held.\n- **Open problems:**\n  - the two values still being refuted, dated 6 October 2026;\n  - the 1,627 dataset classes without a certified pathwidth, and what it would cost to close them;\n  - the lower-bound work, worded only through what the body already covers.\n\n**Left out deliberately:** 